<!-- awf:section positioning -->
# {{ .prefix }}-executing-plans

The inline implementation chain iterates plan phases. Inline is selected per phase, so a mixed plan can hand subagent-driven phases to its companion.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section when-to-invoke -->
## When to invoke

Invoke for an accepted plan with an `inline` phase. Read every phase's declared execution mode; do not infer one mode for the whole plan.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section procedure-resolve-plan -->
## Procedure

<!-- awf:include production-code-outline-approval -->

1. Resolve the mutable plan and read its complete phase structure. This skill may run without an effort. If continuity independently fired, validate its immutable slug and exact `.awf/efforts/<slug>/memory.md`, confirm either legacy `Effort: <slug>` or canonical `effort: <slug>` identity (the legacy form is deprecated and remains only until active efforts finish), preserve one user-managed writer, and use the managed worktree when present; otherwise omit effort and memory fields. If changed facts newly make continuity useful, invoke `{{ if index .skills "effort-workflow" }}{{ .prefix }}-effort-workflow{{ else }}the effort lifecycle owner{{ end }}` before further mutation. A fresh owner assigned one phase or task may run `awf read plan <plan> <P[.T]>` for its executable closure, including the generated task scope notice, phase-owned Advances and Completes outcomes, Notes, and Phase close; all are phase-owner context only; projection changes neither phase ownership nor checkpoint boundaries and never gives a task helper commit, review, checkpoint, handoff, or outcome authority. Repository sources and current-state documentation remain authoritative, and standalone memory is forbidden.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section procedure-raise-concerns -->
2. You, the parent executing this plan, resolve a missing or stale phase path, check, closing subject, or local instruction when repository authority determines it, amend the mutable plan immediately, record the reasoned deviation in Notes, and continue. Use the shared authority boundary when a genuine unresolved design fork remains.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section procedure-per-task -->
3. **Iterate phases, not tasks.** Per `{{ .layout.maintainableCodeDesign }}`, you preserve the plan's settled structural choices, including a bounded enabling refactor; reassess if grounded source contradicts them, stop rather than bolt correctness onto the wrong abstraction, and do not drift from the plan.
<!-- awf:include implementation-autonomy -->
You run `awf context <the phase's exact paths>` before editing.
<!-- awf:include context-spill -->
For an `inline` phase, the parent owns every ordered task, integration, staged check, gate, phase-closing commit, report-only phase review, focused settlement commits, and checkpoint. A batch may dispatch explicitly partitioned sequential commit-disabled helpers only; each is the `implementer` agent in its commit-disabled mode, whose rendered contract governs its path confinement, green obligation, escalation, and return shape. Each brief carries the effort slug and exact owned memory path as read-only context, and helpers never edit shared memory: {{ if .targetSubagentTools }}when operations intentionally target a supplied managed-worktree path, pass that path as `verificationCheckout` while keeping actual mutation paths explicit in the task; verification identity alone targets the worktree, so parent and child Pi CWD remain the project root. Omit `verificationCheckout` for root work. Choose the smallest reliable tier - `small` (narrow, mechanical), `standard` (substantive but bounded), or `large` (broad, intricate, cross-cutting, or high-consequence) - escalating after uncertainty, failed reasoning, or widened scope; omit the `model` field to use configured role routing, overriding deliberately with the tier's exact `provider/model-id`. Never pass `default`, `auto`, or `inherit parent` as a model value. Full tier definitions: docs/working-with-awf.md.{{ else }}choose the smallest reliable tier - `small` (narrow, mechanical), `standard` (substantive but bounded), or `large` (broad, intricate, cross-cutting, or high-consequence) - escalating after uncertainty, failed reasoning, or widened scope; select the smallest reliable target-native model explicitly, or use the harness default and note in the dispatch brief that explicit selection is unavailable. Full tier definitions: docs/working-with-awf.md.{{ end }} You inventory each return, reject out-of-subset writes, retain shared-file ownership, run the deterministic post-check, and never delegate the phase commit. For generated-prose changes, perform the focused meaning review at the produced-output boundaries and retain the inspected boundaries and result as completion evidence. Stage the complete transaction; the phase-closing commit requires `awf check staged` and {{ with .vars.gateCmd }}`{{ . }}`{{ else }}the project's gate{{ end }} to pass - a wired pre-commit hook enforces both at commit time, and a manual run first is needed only in a clone without wired hooks (checkable with `git config core.hooksPath`; when in doubt, run both manually). Before review, build the phase-review brief from the exact phase-closing commit and range, complete phase scope, verification results, the verbatim deviation report built from the parent's inventory, and any unreviewed settlement. Then dispatch report-only phase review through correctness, plan/authority, documentation, and maintainability lenses. Its structured coverage summary returns those exact inputs, the reviewed range, and freshness against the current branch tip. The parent owns this transient evidence, validates branch-tip freshness before reuse, and may persist only a concise advisory observation. Evidence loss after context loss, session replacement, or effort-free continuation, or any unverifiable freshness, falls back to ordinary terminal review. Divergence, changed authority, reasoned post-review fixes, or material mutation invalidates affected coverage and triggers renewed review. Review settles before checkpointing.

<!-- awf:include checkpoint-routine -->
<!-- awf:include phase-loop-continuation -->

If an implementer stops dirty, first inventory `git status --short`, diff, completed and remaining work, and failed checks. Choose exactly one: complete inline; restore the known green baseline and restart the complete revised phase; or stop for required user input. An explicit ownership transfer includes the complete revised phase, inventory, completed and remaining work, and recovery verification. Never issue a blind successor instruction for one task or dispatch over a dirty checkout.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section tdd-opt-in -->
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section gate-tier-detail -->
{{ with .data.e2eSuitePaths }}Use the fuller gate when a phase touches a declared end-to-end surface: {{ range . }}`{{ .path }}` {{ end }}.{{ end }}
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section procedure-adr-final-commit -->
4. Apply every explicit batch of the ADR's declared State changes operations (V2), including the final batch, atomically with its matching claims and lifecycle event in the owning phase. Do not flip terminal artifact status in a phase.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section procedure-non-adr-final-commit -->
5. Keep the plan mutable through implementation; its terminal status changes only in the deferred artifact transaction.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section terminal-step -->
6. After all settled phases, reuse only fresh phase coverage without rereading already-covered phase correctness: a single phase reviews unreviewed settlement or integration, while multiple phases focus cross-phase composition, settlements, and integration. Always retain `awf audit` and `./x audit-local` over the complete final implementation range, including settlement commits. Evaluate implementation-review need independently. Invoke `{{ .prefix }}-reviewing-impl` for meaningful breadth, non-obvious logic, contract or compatibility effects, migrations, security, concurrency, data-loss risk, verification requiring substantive judgment, or uncertainty. Skip it only for locally obvious, low-risk, directly verified work where independent review is unlikely to find meaningful hidden consequences. After assurance settles or is explicitly skipped, effort-backed work returns to `{{ if index .skills "effort-workflow" }}{{ .prefix }}-effort-workflow{{ else }}the effort lifecycle owner{{ end }}` for artifact closure. For effort-free work, the parent performs the deferred ADR/plan terminal transaction: apply only legal status transitions under `{{ .prefix }}-adr-lifecycle`, run the required gate and audit, and then conclude.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section project-invariants -->
## Notes
- Expected output is non-negotiable. Never weaken assertions or adjust a golden merely to pass.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section notes-gate -->
- Gates are mandatory; fix the root cause.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section notes-auto-commit -->
- Auto-commit the phase only when green.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section notes-one-concern -->
- One coherent phase concern per closing commit.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section notes-docs-travel -->
- Docs travel with behavior.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section red-flags -->
## Red flags

Do not turn ordered tasks into transaction boundaries, let helpers commit, or checkpoint before report-only review settlement.
<!-- awf:end -->
